-- rawptr.iron -- RawPtr: type-erased member of the *unchecked T regime
-- Phase 33 STDLIB-10 (Plan 33-06): Iron-side declaration surface for RawPtr.
--
-- RawPtr is the TYPE-ERASED arm of the Phase 25 unchecked regime — a thin
-- handle equivalent to C's `void*` (a bare 8-byte pointer with no generation
-- tracking and no compile-time pointee shape). It coexists with `*unchecked T`
-- (which retains pointee type) and shares the SAME regime guards:
--
--   * Auto-address does NOT apply (Phase 25 UNCK-05: auto_address_applies
--     guard suppresses for `is_unchecked` pointer params).
--   * `&` cannot produce a RawPtr (Phase 25 PTR-05/UNCK-04: E0294 fires when
--     declared type is `*unchecked T` AND the RHS is `&expr`). The only
--     producers of RawPtr / *unchecked T are dedicated compiler builtins:
--     `Box.unwrap()`, `RawPtr.of(x)`, and `Ptr.cast[T](raw)`.
--   * `Ptr.offset` / `Ptr.diff` (Phase 25 UNCK-06, Plan 25-02; shipped) accept
--     any `*unchecked T` first argument; RawPtr inherits transparently.
--
-- The compiler treats `RawPtr` as a TYPE ALIAS for an erased `*unchecked` —
-- internally represented as IRON_TYPE_PTR { is_unchecked=true, pointee=Int }.
-- The Int pointee is a stand-in size (8B); the type-erasure semantics are
-- enforced in the Ptr.cast dispatch which permits casting FROM a RawPtr (or
-- any other `*unchecked T`) TO `*unchecked Target` without the usual pointee-
-- size equality check. Casting BETWEEN checked `*T` regimes still requires
-- size equality (UNCK-04 spec: only the unchecked regime is type-erased).
--
-- RUNTIME REPRESENTATION: 8 bytes (a bare C pointer). NO Iron_FatPtr 16B
-- wrapper, NO generation tag, NO heap-vs-stack discriminator. Dereferencing
-- a RawPtr via `Ptr.cast[T]` and then loading T is UNDEFINED BEHAVIOR if the
-- underlying storage has been freed or did not have type T (DROP-07 / UB
-- contract; see docs/dev/UNCHECKED-LAYOUT.md §4).
--
-- METHODS — all by-name compiler builtins (dispatch in typecheck.c). Their
-- bodies here are intentionally empty: hir_lower skips them (mirrors the
-- box.iron / mutex.iron pattern from Plans 33-01 / 33-05), and the actual
-- value is produced inline by the dispatch arm.
--
-- RawPtr.of(x: T) -> RawPtr
-- Returns a RawPtr pointing at the storage of `x`. The compiler synthesizes
-- a real address-of (bypassing the E0294 `&`-cannot-produce-unchecked guard
-- because `RawPtr.of` IS the address-producer). The address remains valid
-- only while `x`'s storage is live; after-scope use is UB (UNCHECKED-LAYOUT
-- §4). The receiver-form `x.raw_ptr()` is NOT provided — call `RawPtr.of(x)`.
func RawPtr.of[T](x: T) -> RawPtr {}
